var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#RecordIO.jl","page":"RecordIO","text":"RecordIO reads the records of a file one after another, and writes them, with a record parser that finds where each record ends while it finds its fields, so that each byte is read once.\n\nA RecordReader parses the records straight from where their bytes are: one view of them where they are in memory, or can be mapped into it, as a regular file's can, and otherwise the buffer of a BufferIO reader, which it grows as the parser needs, so the parser never sees where one read from the source ends and the next begins. Everything but the source follows from the record type: its parser, which of its parses are records, and its errors. So a reader of an indexed file can answer region queries with the same record type that reads the file from start to end.","title":"RecordIO.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-this-package-covers","page":"RecordIO","text":"Readers: a RecordReader{T} reads records of type T from a source, and is an iterator of them.\nWriters: a RecordWriter{T} writes records of type T to a sink, as the record type writes one.\nRecords: a design of records, of their bytes and where their fields are in them, which own their bytes, as R{Owned}, or borrow those of the reader's buffer, as R{Borrowed}, which allocates nothing for each.\nParsers: a contract for record parsers, a generator of parsers from Automa state machines, and a framing of length-prefixed binary records.\nFields: functions that read the fields of a record's bytes, by the type wanted.\nSources: vectors of bytes and regular files, which the reader parses as one view of their bytes, other IOs, which it buffers with BufferIO, and BufferIO readers, whose buffers it parses from.\n\nIt doesn't parse any format itself: a format's package brings the record type and its parser.","title":"What this package covers"},{"category":"section","location":"#Why-RecordIO","page":"RecordIO","text":"Many of the BioJulia packages that read a file format each carry the same machinery: a buffer over the source, a parser generated by Automa that reads across refills of it, records that copy their bytes out, the errors of malformed records, and a writer. RecordIO is that machinery, written once, with three aims:\n\neach byte of a source is read once, by the parser, which finds where a record ends while it finds its fields;\na record that borrows the reader's buffer allocates nothing, so a query that drops most of the records it reads copies only those it keeps;\none parser and one record type serve a file read from start to end and a region query of its index.","title":"Why RecordIO"},{"category":"section","location":"#How-it-differs-from-other-packages","page":"RecordIO","text":"TranscodingStreams.jl transforms a stream of bytes into another, such as decompressing it; it knows nothing of records. RecordIO turns bytes into records, so the two meet where a decompressing stream is the source of a RecordReader, which it reads with TranscodingStreams loaded.\nAutoma.jl's streams, which many of the BioJulia packages read with, mark where a record starts in the buffer of a TranscodingStream, and copy the record's bytes out of it as the parser reads. A RecordIO parser reads a RecordIO.Stream, whose buffer grows until the whole record is in it and keeps its first byte at position 1, so a record can be a view of the buffer, and a source in memory, or a file mapped into it, is parsed with no copy at all. Any other IO, a TranscodingStream among them, is read into a BufferIO buffer first, which costs a copy of its bytes that a source in memory and a BufferIO reader don't.\nBioGenerics.jl's AbstractReader and AbstractWriter are interfaces: open, the do-block form, and iteration by read! into a mutable record, with the reading left to each format. RecordIO does the reading: a RecordReader{T} is the iterator, and RecordIO.AbstractReader{T} and RecordIO.AbstractWriter{T} are typed by their records, so that a package's Reader{Record{Borrowed}} reads views and Reader{Record{Owned}} copies, with offsets in the source, the bytes after the records, and checks of records against their specification.\nBufferIO.jl is the buffer that RecordIO parses from where the bytes aren't in memory, and its readers are sources of a RecordReader as they are.","title":"How it differs from other packages"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"RecordIO","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"RecordIO\")\n\nThe versions of Julia and of its dependencies that RecordIO supports are the compat bounds of its Project.toml.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-manual","page":"RecordIO","text":"Record types describes the design of records, what a record type says by its methods, the parser, generating parsers with Automa, length-prefixed records, checking records against a specification, and where a reader is in its source.\nReading records describes readers, owned records and views, which bytes each part owns, the readers of a package, and the bytes after the records.\nSources describes what a reader reads from, and how to make a source of your own.\nErrors describes what a reader throws when a parser fails.\nThe Reference lists every documented function and type.","title":"The manual"},{"category":"section","location":"#First-steps","page":"RecordIO","text":"A record type, Line, is one line of text, which its parser, a method of RecordIO.read_record, reads, giving nothing for a comment; Record types shows both. A reader of a few lines, which skips the comments:\n\nreader = RecordReader{Line{Owned}}(IOBuffer(\"# a header\\nfirst\\nsecond\\n\"))\n[String(text(line)) for line in reader]\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"first\"\n \"second\"\n\nA Line{Owned} owns its bytes, so it stays as it is after the next record is read, and a LineView, a Line{Borrowed}, borrows those of the reader's buffer. RecordReader is exported; the other functions are public, and are written here with their module, as RecordIO.remaining.","title":"First steps"},{"category":"section","location":"parsers/#Record-types","page":"Record types","text":"A record type is a package's own: an immutable value of the bytes of a record and where its fields are in them.\n\nstruct Record{O <: RecordIO.Ownership}\n    bytes::ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}\n    fields::Fields\nend\n\nOwnership: RecordIO.Ownership is a parameter of the type: a Record{Owned} owns its bytes, and a Record{Borrowed}, a view, borrows those of the reader's buffer, so it allocates nothing, and is valid until the next record is read.\nFields: where the fields are, such as ranges of the bytes, which the parser finds as it reads the record, so that an accessor reads a field without finding it again.\nCopies: copy of a view is an owned record, with a copy of the bytes, and since a record is immutable, copy of an owned record is the record itself.","title":"Record types"},{"category":"section","location":"parsers/#What-a-record-type-says","page":"Record types","text":"A reader takes only the source: everything else follows from the record type, by its methods.\n\nMethod What it says Default\nRecordIO.read_record(T, stream) the parser of a record that of its views, copied, for an owned type\nRecordIO.owned_type(T) the records that own their bytes of the views T, which collect gives T\nRecordIO.borrowed_type(T) the views of the owned records T, which a reader that drops some records reads T\nRecordIO.malformation(T, stream) why a record is malformed that of its views, or ArgumentError(\"the record is malformed\")\nRecordIO.exception(T) the errors of its records RecordIO.RecordError","title":"What a record type says"},{"category":"section","location":"parsers/#The-parser","page":"Record types","text":"A record parser reads one record at a time from a RecordIO.Stream, the bytes of the reader's buffer from the first byte of the record:\n\nRecordIO.read_record(::Type{T}, stream::RecordIO.Stream) -> (x, n::Int)\n\nx is the record, of type T, or what the parser found that isn't one, such as nothing for a comment, which the reader skips.\nn > 0 is the number of bytes that the record took, its end, such as a newline, included.\nn < 0 says that the record is malformed, and RecordIO.malformation says why, so that reading a record enters no try.\nThe stream is iterated as a vector is: iterate(stream, p) gives the byte at position p, from 1, and reads more of the source when p passes the bytes in the buffer, so the parser never sees where one read from the source ends and the next begins.\nRecordIO.bytes(stream) is a view of the bytes read so far, of which a view of the record borrows its own.\n\nA line of text, which the other pages of the manual read, is a record without fields. Its parser finds the newline with RecordIO.skip_while, which tests 32 bytes of a stream at a time:\n\nusing RecordIO\nusing RecordIO: Ownership, Owned, Borrowed\nusing MemoryViews: ImmutableMemoryView\nusing StringViews: StringView\n\nstruct Line{O <: Ownership}\n    bytes::ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}\nend\n\nconst LineView = Line{Borrowed}\n\ntext(line::Line) = StringView(line.bytes)\n\nBase.copy(line::LineView) = Line{Owned}(ImmutableMemoryView(collect(line.bytes)))\nBase.copy(line::Line{Owned}) = line\nRecordIO.owned_type(::Type{LineView}) = Line{Owned}\nRecordIO.borrowed_type(::Type{Line{Owned}}) = LineView\n\n@inline function RecordIO.read_record(::Type{LineView}, stream::RecordIO.Stream)\n    p = RecordIO.skip_while(!=(UInt8('\\n')), stream, 1)\n    data = RecordIO.bytes(stream)\n    # At the end of the source, `p` is after the last byte.\n    taken = min(p, length(data))\n    n = data[taken] == UInt8('\\n') ? taken - 1 : taken\n    is_line = n > 0 && data[1] != UInt8('#')\n    return (is_line ? LineView(data[1:n]) : nothing), taken\nend\n\n[String(text(line)) for line in RecordReader{LineView}(IOBuffer(\"# a header\\nfirst\\nsecond\"))]\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"first\"\n \"second\"\n\nA parser that returns a record or something else, such as nothing, is @inline, so that the reader splits the two where it calls it, rather than allocating for each record. The parser of the owned records follows from that of the views: since borrowed_type(Line{Owned}) is LineView, a reader of Line{Owned} reads views and copies each.\n\nRecordIO.parse_record(T, data) parses all of data as one record, as a package makes a record of given bytes:\n\nString(text(RecordIO.parse_record(Line{Owned}, Vector{UInt8}(\"given\\n\"))))\n\"given\"","title":"The parser"},{"category":"section","location":"parsers/#Fields","page":"Record types","text":"The fields of a record are ranges of its bytes, which these read by the type wanted, allocating only for a String:\n\nRecordIO.parse_field(T, data, range): an integer, a float, a String, a StringView or a Char;\nRecordIO.is_missing(data, range, placeholder): whether a field is the placeholder of a missing value;\nRecordIO.separated(data, range, delimiter): the ranges of the values of a field;\nRecordIO.field_equals(data, range, text): whether a field is a text.\n\ndata = Vector{UInt8}(\"chr1\\t-12\\t3.5e2\\ta,b\")\nRecordIO.parse_field(Int, data, 6:8), RecordIO.parse_field(Float64, data, 10:14), [String(data[value]) for value in RecordIO.separated(data, 16:18, UInt8(','))]\n(-12, 350.0, [\"a\", \"b\"])","title":"Fields"},{"category":"section","location":"parsers/#Parsers-from-Automa-machines","page":"Record types","text":"With Automa loaded, RecordIO.generate_read_record generates the parser of a record type from a state machine, and its malformation, which parses a malformed record again to say where it fails. The machine matches one record, or what isn't a record, and what ends it; its actions find the fields in local variables, and the expression record makes the record of them once it is complete.\n\nusing Automa\n\nBase.@kwdef struct ItemFields\n    name::UnitRange{Int} = 1:0\n    number::UnitRange{Int} = 1:0\nend\n\nstruct Item{O <: Ownership}\n    bytes::ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}\n    fields::ItemFields\nend\n\nname(item::Item) = RecordIO.parse_field(StringView, item.bytes, item.fields.name)\nnumber(item::Item) = RecordIO.parse_field(Int, item.bytes, item.fields.number)\n\n# A name and a number, or a comment, to a newline or the end of the source.\nconst item_machine = let\n    name = onexit!(onenter!(re\"[a-z][a-z0-9]*\", :mark), :name)\n    number = onexit!(onenter!(re\"[0-9]+\", :mark), :number)\n    item = onexit!(name * re\"\\t\" * number, :item)\n    comment = re\"#[^\\n]*\"\n    compile((item | comment) * opt(onfinal!(re\"\\n\", :end_of_record)))\nend\n\nconst item_actions = Dict(\n    :mark => :(mark = p),\n    :name => :(name = mark:(p - 1)),\n    :number => :(number = mark:(p - 1)),\n    :item => :(filled = p - 1),\n)\n\n@eval $(\n    RecordIO.generate_read_record(\n        Item{Borrowed}, item_machine, item_actions;\n        setup = :(mark = 0; filled = 0; name = number = 1:0),\n        record = :(iszero(filled) ? nothing : Item{Borrowed}(data[1:filled], ItemFields(; name, number))),\n    )\n)\n\n[(String(name(item)), number(item)) for item in RecordReader{Item{Borrowed}}(IOBuffer(\"# items\\nfirst\\t1\\nsecond\\t22\\n\"))]\n2-element Vector{Tuple{String, Int64}}:\n (\"first\", 1)\n (\"second\", 22)\n\nThe machine takes each byte from the stream, so each byte is read once, and only the end of the source is the end of its input. With skip = true, a state that loops to itself on a set of bytes skips them at once, which pays for fields with long runs of such bytes.","title":"Parsers from Automa machines"},{"category":"section","location":"parsers/#Length-prefixed-records","page":"Record types","text":"RecordIO.LengthPrefixed frames binary records that start with their length, as BAM records do, with a decoder of one record, which returns the exception of a malformed record rather than throwing it:\n\nstruct Blob{O <: Ownership}\n    bytes::ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}\nend\n\n# The payload follows the 4 bytes of the length.\ndecode_blob(::Type{Blob{Borrowed}}, bytes) = length(bytes) > 4 ? Blob{Borrowed}(bytes[5:end]) : ArgumentError(\"a blob without a payload\")\n\nRecordIO.read_record(::Type{Blob{Borrowed}}, stream::RecordIO.Stream) = RecordIO.LengthPrefixed(decode_blob)(Blob{Borrowed}, stream)\nRecordIO.malformation(::Type{Blob{Borrowed}}, stream::RecordIO.Stream) = RecordIO.malformation(RecordIO.LengthPrefixed(decode_blob), Blob{Borrowed}, stream)\n\nblob(payload) = [reinterpret(UInt8, [htol(Int32(sizeof(payload)))]); codeunits(payload)]\n[String(blob.bytes) for blob in RecordReader{Blob{Borrowed}}(IOBuffer([blob(\"first\"); blob(\"second\")]))]\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"first\"\n \"second\"","title":"Length-prefixed records"},{"category":"section","location":"parsers/#Checking-records-against-a-specification","page":"Record types","text":"A parser finds the fields of a record by its syntax, and a reader yields what it finds. What the specification of a format says of the values of the fields, such as their ranges, and how they agree with each other, is checked when it is asked for, when a file is new, rather than as part of reading it every time.\n\nA record package says how a record breaks the specification, with a method of RecordIO.problem, which reads the fields as their accessors do, and a number with RecordIO.try_parse_field, which gives nothing where the accessor's RecordIO.parse_field throws. RecordIO.is_valid and RecordIO.validate of a record follow from it, and RecordIO.description is what an error quotes of a record.\n\n# How an item breaks the rule that its number is at most 255, or `nothing`.\nRecordIO.problem(item::Item) = RecordIO.try_parse_field(UInt8, item.bytes, item.fields.number) === nothing ? \"the number is above 255\" : nothing\nRecordIO.description(item::Item) = RecordIO.description(item.bytes)\n\nitems = collect(RecordReader{Item{Borrowed}}(IOBuffer(\"first\\t1\\nsecond\\t256\\n\")))\nmap(RecordIO.is_valid, items), RecordIO.problem(last(items))\n(Bool[1, 0], \"the number is above 255\")\n\nThe check of a file is a loop over a reader, RecordIO.validate(step, reader, state), whose step checks each record with what those before it left in state, for what the specification says of a file as a whole. Its error is that of the record, with its number and its offset in the source, as the reader throws for a malformed one.\n\n# Each item honours the specification, and the numbers of a file do not descend.\nfunction step(last_number, item)\n    message = RecordIO.problem(item)\n    message === nothing || return message\n    return number(item) >= last_number ? number(item) : \"the numbers descend\"\nend\n\ntry\n    RecordIO.validate(step, RecordReader{Item{Borrowed}}(IOBuffer(\"first\\t1\\nsecond\\t256\\n\")), 0)\ncatch error\n    error\nend\nRecordIO.RecordError{Int64}(8, 2, ArgumentError(\"the number is above 255 in the record \\\"second\\\\t256\\\"\"))\n\nA package's reader checks its file with a method of RecordIO.validate(reader) that calls the loop, and RecordIO.validate(Reader, source) opens a source with a type of reader, checks it, and closes it.","title":"Checking records against a specification"},{"category":"section","location":"parsers/#Where-a-reader-is","page":"Record types","text":"RecordIO.offset(reader) is where the record last read starts in the source, RecordIO.next_offset(reader) where the next one starts, and RecordIO.number(reader) the number of parses so far, which is the line number of a text file of one record a line. An offset is a number of bytes, or the source's own kind of offset, as a source of a BGZF file gives virtual offsets.\n\nreader = RecordReader{Item{Borrowed}}(IOBuffer(\"# items\\nfirst\\t1\\nsecond\\t22\\n\"))\n[(String(name(item)), RecordIO.number(reader), RecordIO.offset(reader), RecordIO.next_offset(reader)) for item in reader]\n2-element Vector{Tuple{String, Int64, Int64, Int64}}:\n (\"first\", 2, 8, 16)\n (\"second\", 3, 16, 26)","title":"Where a reader is"},{"category":"section","location":"writing/#Writing-records","page":"Writing records","text":"A RecordWriter{T} writes records of type T to a sink, and is the mirror of a RecordReader{T}. A record type says how one record is written with a method of RecordIO.write_record, as it says how one is read with RecordIO.read_record. The Line record type of Record types is written as its bytes and a newline:\n\nRecordIO.write_record(sink, line::Line) = RecordIO.write_line(sink, line.bytes)\n\nwrite writes a record, several, or all those of a vector or of a reader, and returns the number of bytes written. The records of a reader of views are each written before the next is read, so copying a file allocates nothing for each record:\n\ntext_of_lines = join([\"first\", \"second\", \"third\"], '\\n') * '\\n'\noutput = IOBuffer()\nwriter = RecordWriter{Line}(output)\nwrite(writer, RecordReader{LineView}(IOBuffer(text_of_lines)))\n19\n\nThe writer counts the records and the bytes that it has written:\n\nRecordIO.number(writer), RecordIO.offset(writer), String(take!(output))\n(3, 19, \"first\\nsecond\\nthird\\n\")\n\nThe writer trusts its records, and writes them as they are. What the specification of a format says of a record is checked when it is asked for, with RecordIO.validate.","title":"Writing records"},{"category":"section","location":"writing/#Sinks","page":"Writing records","text":"A sink is an IO or a BufferIO writer, and close(writer) closes it. Given a function, a writer is closed when the function returns or throws, and open makes a writer of a file, to write it or, with append = true, to add to it:\n\npath = tempname()\nopen(RecordWriter{Line}, path) do writer\n    write(writer, collect(RecordReader{Line{Owned}}(IOBuffer(text_of_lines))))\nend\nread(path, String)\n\"first\\nsecond\\nthird\\n\"","title":"Sinks"},{"category":"section","location":"writing/#What-a-record-type-writes","page":"Writing records","text":"write_record(sink, record) writes one record with what ends or frames it, and returns the number of bytes. These write to any sink, and allocate nothing:\n\nRecordIO.write_bytes: a vector or a memory view of bytes, or a range of it;\nRecordIO.write_line: the same, and a newline;\nRecordIO.write_le: an integer, in little-endian order, as binary formats store them;\nRecordIO.write_length_prefixed: bytes after their length, the framing that RecordIO.LengthPrefixed reads.\n\nA method of write_record for anything else that a file holds, such as a line of a header, is written by the same write, and is counted in the bytes of the writer, but not in its records.","title":"What a record type writes"},{"category":"section","location":"writing/#Writers-of-record-packages","page":"Writing records","text":"A package's writer is a subtype of RecordIO.AbstractWriter{T} that holds a RecordWriter{T} in a field named records, as its reader holds a RecordReader{T}. It needs only a constructor that takes the sink first, where it may write a header, and has write, close, flush, open and the form that takes a function from its supertype:\n\nstruct LineWriter{S} <: RecordIO.AbstractWriter{Line}\n    records::RecordWriter{Line, S}\nend\n\nLineWriter(sink) = LineWriter(RecordWriter{Line}(sink))\n\noutput = IOBuffer()\nwriter = LineWriter(output)\nwrite(writer, RecordIO.parse_record(LineView, codeunits(\"only\")))\nString(take!(output))\n\"only\\n\"","title":"Writers of record packages"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Reference","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"module","location":"reference/#RecordIO.RecordIO","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO\n\nRead the records of a source one after another, with a record parser that finds where each record ends while it finds its fields, so that each byte is read once.\n\nA RecordReader{T} yields records of type T, which a record package defines, with RecordIO.read_record(T, stream), its parser, which makes each record. The reader manages the stream of bytes: it parses straight from where they are, one view of them where they are in memory, or can be mapped into it, and otherwise the buffer of a BufferIO reader, which grows when the parser asks for more bytes, so the parser never sees where one read from the source ends and the next begins. Sources are vectors of bytes, IOs, which it maps into memory where they are regular files and otherwise buffers with a BufReader, and BufferIO readers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.RecordIO"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#RecordIO.AbstractReader","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.AbstractReader{T}\n\nThe supertype of the readers of records of type T: RecordReader{T} itself, and the readers of a record package, which wrap one, RecordIO.records(reader), by default their field records:\n\niterating yields its records of type T;\neltype is T;\ncollect gives records of RecordIO.owned_type(T);\nRecordIO.offset, RecordIO.next_offset, RecordIO.number and RecordIO.record_error are those of the RecordReader it wraps.\n\nA package's reader is Reader{T, …} <: RecordIO.AbstractReader{T}, so that Reader{Record{Borrowed}} reads views, as a Vector{T} holds elements of type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.AbstractReader"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#RecordIO.AbstractWriter","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.AbstractWriter{T}\n\nThe supertype of the writers of records of type T: RecordWriter{T} itself, and the writers of a record package, which wrap one, as its readers wrap a RecordReader, the mirror of RecordIO.AbstractReader.\n\nA subtype has its RecordWriter{T} in a field named records, or defines RecordIO.records for it, and a constructor that takes the sink first. It then writes with write, is closed with close and flushed with flush, and has:\n\nW(f::Function, sink, args...), which calls f with the writer and closes it afterwards;\nopen(W, path, args...; append = false) and open(f, W, path, args...; append = false), which open the file at path to write it, or to append to it;\nRecordIO.number and RecordIO.offset, the records and the bytes it has written.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.AbstractWriter"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#RecordIO.Borrowed","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.Borrowed\n\nThe RecordIO.Ownership of a record that borrows the bytes of the buffer it was parsed from, which are valid until the next record is read from the source.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.Borrowed"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#RecordIO.LengthPrefixed","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.LengthPrefixed(decode)\n\nA parser of length-prefixed binary records, such as BAM records, of a decoder of one record, for a package's RecordIO.read_record:\n\nRecordIO.read_record(::Type{Record{Borrowed}}, stream) = RecordIO.LengthPrefixed(decode)(Record{Borrowed}, stream)\nRecordIO.malformation(::Type{Record{Borrowed}}, stream) = RecordIO.malformation(RecordIO.LengthPrefixed(decode), Record{Borrowed}, stream)\n\nEach record starts with its length in bytes as a little-endian Int32, not counting the 4 bytes of the length itself (block_size in BAM). decode(T, bytes) decodes the record of type T from bytes, a view of its bytes, length included, which a record of type R{Borrowed} may borrow. For a malformed record, it returns the exception that says why, rather than throwing it, so that reading a record enters no try; anything else it returns is the record. A negative length is malformed too, and a record that the source ends inside is truncated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.LengthPrefixed"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#RecordIO.MalformedRecord","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.MalformedRecord{C}(prefix::String)\n\nAn error of a record package: a function of (offset, number, cause), as RecordIO.exception returns, that turns the failure of its parser into the package's own error.\n\nA cause of a type in C, such as ArgumentError, becomes ArgumentError(\"$(prefix) $(number) at offset $(offset): $(message)\"), as in MalformedRecord{ArgumentError}(\"Malformed record\"), and any other cause a RecordIO.RecordError. The offset is the record's in the source, as RecordIO.offset gives it, such as a virtual offset in a query of an indexed file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.MalformedRecord"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#RecordIO.Owned","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.Owned\n\nThe RecordIO.Ownership of a record that owns its bytes, in memory of its own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.Owned"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#RecordIO.Ownership","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.Ownership\n\nWhether a record owns its bytes, RecordIO.Owned, or borrows those of the buffer it was parsed from, RecordIO.Borrowed, as the parameter of a record type of a package's own, such as Record{O <: RecordIO.Ownership}.\n\nA Record{Borrowed} is a view of the bytes, which borrow the buffer of the reader's source, and is only valid until the next record is read from the source; a Record{Owned} holds a copy of them, and stays valid as long as it is kept. The package defines both, how a view becomes an owned record, with copy, and RecordIO.owned_type and RecordIO.borrowed_type, which pair them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.Ownership"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#RecordIO.RecordError","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.RecordError(offset, number, exception)\n\nThrown when a record parser finds a record malformed, or when the source ends inside a record.\n\noffset: where the record starts in the source, as RecordIO.offset gives it; by default, the number of bytes of the source before it, counted from where reading started.\nnumber: the number of the parse, counting those read before it, what isn't a record, such as a comment, included.\nexception: the cause, which RecordIO.malformation gives, or what describes an incomplete record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.RecordError"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#RecordIO.RecordReader","page":"Reference","text":"RecordReader{T}(source)\nRecordReader{T}(reader::RecordReader)\n\nRead the records of type T of source one after another, each made by RecordIO.read_record(T, stream), a parser that the package of T defines, with its errors, RecordIO.exception(T).\n\nsource is a vector of bytes that are contiguous in memory, such as a Vector{UInt8} or a contiguous view of one, an IO, or a BufferIO reader. Where its bytes are in memory, as a vector's and a CursorReader's are, or can be mapped into memory, as a regular file's can, the reader parses them as one view; otherwise it parses from a BufferIO buffer that grows: a BufReader of an IO, or the buffer of a BufferIO reader, wrapped with expandable if it can't grow. To choose the size of the buffer, pass a BufReader(io, size). The reader takes over its source: read from it only through the reader, or through RecordIO.remaining.\n\nA reader is an iterator of records of type T, as a Vector{T} is of its elements:\n\nit yields what the parser makes that is a T, and skips the rest, such as comments;\na record that borrows the buffer of the source, such as an R{Borrowed}, is valid until the next record is read;\ncollect gives records of RecordIO.owned_type(T), copies of those that borrow;\niteration ends at the end of the source, with nothing, as an iterator does;\nclose(reader) closes the source.\n\nIt is itself a RecordIO.AbstractReader{T}, whose RecordIO.records is itself.\n\nMade from another reader, it carries on from where that reader is, through its source and its buffer, with its count of records and of bytes, and reads records of type T in place of that reader's, which is not to be read from again. So a package reads the rest of a source as another type, such as one that keeps the lines that its records skip, to check a file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.RecordReader"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#RecordIO.RecordWriter","page":"Reference","text":"RecordWriter{T}(sink)\nRecordWriter{T}(f::Function, sink)\n\nA writer of records of type T to sink, an IO or a BufferIO writer, the mirror of RecordReader{T}.\n\nwrite(writer, record) writes a record as RecordIO.write_record of its type does, and returns the number of bytes written; it takes several records too, and a vector or a reader of them. Anything else that write_record writes, such as the lines of a header, is written the same, and is counted in the bytes of the writer but not in its records. The writer trusts its records: what a format's specification says of them is checked when it is asked for, with RecordIO.validate.\n\nClosing the writer closes its sink, and given a function f, the writer is passed to f and closed afterwards.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.RecordWriter"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#RecordIO.Stop","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.Stop()\n\nWhat the step of RecordIO.validate returns where the records end before the source does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.Stop"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#RecordIO.Stream","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.Stream\n\nThe bytes that a record parser reads: an iterator over the bytes of the reader's buffer, from the first byte of a record, as UInt8s.\n\nAs for a vector, iterate(stream, p) gives the byte at position p, from 1, so a parser's position is the state it iterates with, and enumerate(stream) gives positions and bytes.\nWhen p passes the bytes in the buffer, the stream reads more of the source, and it ends at the end of the source, so a parser never sees where one read from the source ends and the next begins.\nRecordIO.bytes(stream) is an ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8} of the bytes read so far, of which a record takes its own.\n\nThe bytes start at the record's first byte, which stays at index 1 when they grow, so a parser's positions stay valid. A record may borrow them, which are valid until the reader reads the next record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.Stream"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.borrowed_type-Union{Tuple{Type{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.borrowed_type(T) -> Type\n\nThe type of the views of the records of type T, which borrow their bytes, so that a reader that drops some of the records it reads, such as a query of a region, reads views and copies only those it keeps. By default, it is T, whose records have no views; a package defines it for its owned records, as RecordIO.borrowed_type(::Type{Record{Owned}}) = Record{Borrowed}, the reverse of RecordIO.owned_type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.borrowed_type"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.bytes-Tuple{RecordIO.Stream}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.bytes(stream::RecordIO.Stream) -> ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}\n\nThe bytes of stream read so far, from the first byte of the record that the parser reads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.bytes"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.copy_bytes-Tuple{MemoryViews.ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.copy_bytes(bytes::ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}) -> ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}\n\nA copy of the bytes of a record, in memory of its own, for the owned copy of a view, which a record package's copy makes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.copy_bytes"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.description-Tuple{AbstractVector{UInt8}}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.description(bytes::AbstractVector{UInt8}) -> String\n\nThe bytes of a record, quoted, and cut after 100 bytes, as the errors of this package quote a record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.description"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.description-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.description(record) -> String\n\nWhat the error of a record that breaks the specification quotes of it, such as its line; by default nothing. A record package defines it for its record types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.description"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.exception-Tuple{Type}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.exception(T)\n\nThe errors of the records of type T: a function of (offset, number, cause) that returns the exception to throw for a malformed record, such as a RecordIO.MalformedRecord. By default, it is RecordIO.RecordError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.exception"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.field_equals-Tuple{AbstractVector{UInt8}, UnitRange{Int64}, AbstractString}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.field_equals(data::AbstractVector{UInt8}, range::UnitRange{Int}, text::AbstractString) -> Bool\n\nWhether the field of data in range is text, allocating nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.field_equals"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#RecordIO.generate_execute_code","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.generate_execute_code(machine::Automa.Machine, actions::Dict{Symbol}; iterator = :iterator, skip = false) -> Expr\n\nGenerate the code of machine with actions, which takes each byte from the iterator of bytes in the variable named iterator, and whose end is the end of the input; this needs Automa to be loaded.\n\nThe machine knows nothing of where the bytes come from: a Vector{UInt8}, a view, or a RecordIO.Stream, which reads more of the source as it goes, whose iterate(iterator, p) gives the byte at position p. The code is built from the pieces of Automa's goto generator, and has its variables:\n\np, the position of the byte, from 1, during its actions, and the position after the last byte at the end of the input;\nbyte, the byte, and cs, the state, which is 0 after the input was accepted at its end, negative when the machine failed, at state -cs, and positive after @escape.\n\nActions may use @escape, which stops the machine after the byte, with p at the next byte. At the end of the input, a machine in a final state runs its actions at the end, and any other rejects the input.\n\nWith skip = true, a state that loops to itself on a set of bytes, with no actions, skips the bytes of the set with RecordIO.skip_while, when the byte after the state's first is in the set too. It pays for fields with long runs of such bytes, such as the sequences of SAM records, and costs for fields of a few bytes each, such as the attributes of GFF3 records, so a package measures its records both ways.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.generate_execute_code"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#RecordIO.generate_read_record","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.generate_read_record(T, machine::Automa.Machine, actions::Dict{Symbol}; record, kwargs...) -> Expr\n\nGenerate the definitions of RecordIO.read_record(::Type{T}, stream), a parser of records of type T with an Automa machine, and of its RecordIO.malformation. Define them with @eval; this needs Automa to be loaded.\n\nThe machine takes each byte from the stream, which reads more of the source as it goes, so each byte is read once, the machine knows nothing of where one read from the source ends and the next begins, and only the end of the source is the end of its input; its code is RecordIO.generate_execute_code's. It returns (x, n): the value of the expression record and the bytes the record took, when it is complete, or (nothing, -1) if the machine fails. malformation runs the machine again with checking = true to throw the error of a malformed record, so that parsing a well-formed record enters no try.\n\nmachine matches one record, or what isn't a record, such as a comment, and what ends it, which must be optional so that the last record of a source may lack it. The action named endaction marks the end of a record: attach it to the final byte of what ends it, as in opt(onfinal!(re\"\\n\", :end_of_record)) for records that end with a newline. The generated code defines the action, so actions must not, and stops the machine there. A record also ends at the end of the source when the machine accepts all of its bytes.\n\nCode in actions and setup sees the position p of the machine, its state cs, the byte, and checking, which is true only when malformation parses a malformed record again. record and error see data too, the bytes that the machine read, which actions don't, since they come from the stream as the machine reads. When the record is complete, p - 1 is its number of bytes, so record finds it in data[1:(p - 1)], whose view a record of type R{Borrowed} may borrow. To reject a record that the machine accepts, record returns (nothing, -1), and with checking throws the reason.\n\nKeyword arguments:\n\nrecord: the expression of the record of type T, or of what isn't one, such as nothing for a comment.\nsetup: code run before the machine, such as defining the variables that actions assign.\nendaction = :end_of_record: the name of the action that marks the end of a record.\nskip = false: whether states that loop to themselves skip runs of bytes at once, as RecordIO.generate_execute_code does with skip = true.\nerror: code run if the machine fails when malformation parses the record again, which should throw; by default RecordIO.malformed_record(data, p), which quotes the record and gives the position p of the failing byte. An ArgumentError that record throws while checking gains the quoted record too.\n\nusing Automa, RecordIO, MemoryViews\nusing RecordIO: Owned, Borrowed\n\n# A record of one name, which borrows its bytes, or owns them.\nstruct Name{O <: RecordIO.Ownership}\n    bytes::ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}\nend\n\nconst machine = let\n    name = onexit!(onenter!(re\"[^#\\n][^\\n]*\", :mark), :name)\n    comment = re\"#[^\\n]*\"\n    compile((name | comment) * opt(onfinal!(re\"\\n\", :end_of_record)))\nend\n\nconst actions = Dict(:mark => :(mark = p), :name => :(range = mark:(p - 1)))\n\n# A comment is `nothing`, which the reader skips.\n@eval $(RecordIO.generate_read_record(Name{Borrowed}, machine, actions;\n    setup = :(mark = 0; range = 1:0),\n    record = :(isempty(range) ? nothing : Name{Borrowed}(data[range])),\n))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.generate_read_record"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.is_missing-Tuple{AbstractVector{UInt8}, UnitRange{Int64}, UInt8}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.is_missing(data::AbstractVector{UInt8}, range::UnitRange{Int}, placeholder::UInt8) -> Bool\n\nWhether the field of data in range is the placeholder of a missing value, one byte of placeholder, such as . in BED and GFF3 and * in SAM.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.is_missing"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.is_valid-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.is_valid(record) -> Bool\n\nWhether record honours the specification of its format, as RecordIO.problem tells.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.is_valid"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.malformation-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, RecordIO.Stream}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.malformation(::Type{T}, stream::RecordIO.Stream) -> Exception\n\nWhy the record of type T at the start of stream is malformed, after RecordIO.read_record(T, stream) returned a negative count: the cause that the reader's exception turns into its error.\n\nIt is called only for a malformed record, so it may parse the record again to say why, and may throw the cause rather than return it. By default, it is that of the views of T, RecordIO.borrowed_type(T), where T has them, and otherwise ArgumentError(\"the record is malformed\"). Parsers made by RecordIO.generate_read_record define it, with the messages of their errors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.malformation"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.malformed_record-Tuple{AbstractVector{UInt8}, Int64}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.malformed_record(data::AbstractVector{UInt8}, p::Int) -> ArgumentError\n\nThe error of a record at the start of data that a parser fails at byte p of: the record from its start to 20 bytes past p, cut after 100 bytes, and p, as the record \"chr1\\tx\\t5\" does not match the format at byte 6. It is the default error of RecordIO.generate_read_record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.malformed_record"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.next_offset-Tuple{RecordReader}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.next_offset(reader) -> offset\n\nWhere the next record of reader starts in its source, which is where the reader is: after the record that it last read. It is an offset as RecordIO.offset(reader) is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.next_offset"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.number-Tuple{RecordIO.AbstractWriter}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.number(writer) -> Int\n\nThe number of records that writer has written.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.number"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.number-Tuple{RecordReader}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.number(reader) -> Int\n\nThe number of parses that reader has made, what isn't a record, such as a comment, included: the number of the record that it last read, which is its line number in a text file of one record a line, read from its start.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.number"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.offset-Tuple{Any, Int64}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.offset(source, bytes::Int)\n\nWhere a record starts in source, for the error of a malformed record: by default bytes, the number of bytes of the source before it, counted from where reading started.\n\nsource is the source that the RecordReader was made with, so a package adds a method for its type of source to give its own kind of offset instead, such as the virtual offset of a BGZF file. It is called only when a record fails, so it costs nothing per record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.offset"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.offset-Tuple{RecordIO.AbstractWriter}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.offset(writer) -> Int\n\nThe number of bytes that writer has written to its sink, which is the offset of the next record from where the writer began.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.offset"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.offset-Tuple{RecordReader}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.offset(reader) -> offset\n\nWhere the record that reader last read starts in its source, as RecordIO.offset(source, bytes) gives it: by default the number of bytes of the source before it, counted from where reading started, or the source's own kind of offset, such as the virtual offset of a BGZF file. Before the first record, and after reading from RecordIO.remaining, it is where the reader is. reader is a RecordReader, or a package's RecordIO.AbstractReader.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.offset"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.owned_type-Union{Tuple{Type{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.owned_type(T) -> Type\n\nThe type of the records that own their bytes of the records of type T, which collect of a reader of T gives, by copy of each. By default, it is T, whose records are their own; a package defines it for the views of its records, as RecordIO.owned_type(::Type{Record{Borrowed}}) = Record{Owned}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.owned_type"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#RecordIO.parse_field","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.parse_field(T, data::AbstractVector{UInt8}, range::UnitRange{Int}) -> T\n\nThe field of data in range, as a T, which allocates nothing but for a String:\n\nT The field\nT <: Unsigned a decimal of [0-9]+\nT <: Signed a decimal of [-+]?[0-9]+\nT <: AbstractFloat a decimal or scientific number, as parse reads it\nString a copy of the bytes\nStringView a view of the bytes\nChar its one byte\n\nIntegers are read as the parser of the record matched them, so their digits aren't checked, but their overflow is, with an OverflowError, and every value of T can be read, its least included. A float that isn't one, and an integer without digits, such as an empty field or a sign alone, throw an ArgumentError, and a range outside data throws as indexing data there does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.parse_field"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.parse_record-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, AbstractVector{UInt8}}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.parse_record(::Type{T}, data::AbstractVector{UInt8}) -> T\n\nParse all of data as one record of type T, with RecordIO.read_record(T, stream), as the end of a source, and return it; a record of type R{Borrowed} borrows data. It throws the record's malformation if it is malformed, and an ArgumentError if it is truncated, if it isn't a T, such as a comment, or if bytes are left after it. It is how a package makes a record of given bytes, as a constructor Record(data) does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.parse_record"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#RecordIO.problem","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.problem(record) -> Union{Nothing, String}\n\nHow record breaks the specification of its format, or nothing if it honours it. A record package defines it for its record types, by reading the fields of the record as its accessors do, with RecordIO.try_parse_field for its numbers; RecordIO.is_valid and RecordIO.validate follow from it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.problem"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.read_next-Union{Tuple{RecordReader{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.read_next(reader::RecordReader{T}) -> Union{T, Nothing}\n\nThe next record of reader, skipping what isn't one, or nothing at the end of the source. It is how a package reads records itself, as a query of an indexed file does, and iterate reads through it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.read_next"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#RecordIO.read_record","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.read_record(::Type{T}, stream::RecordIO.Stream) -> (x, n::Int)\n\nParse one record of type T from the start of RecordIO.bytes(stream), and return it, x, with the number of bytes it took, n, its end, such as a newline, included. A record package defines it for its record types, and a RecordReader{T} calls it for each record.\n\nx is the record, of type T, or what the parser found that isn't one, such as nothing for a comment or a blank line, or a record of another kind, which the reader skips: it yields only an x isa T.\nn > 0 is the bytes of the record, which the reader consumes before it reads the next one, so that a record of type T may borrow them till then.\nn < 0 says the record is malformed, and RecordIO.malformation(T, stream) says why, so that reading a record enters no try, which would cost more than a short record takes to parse.\nThe parser iterates over the bytes of the stream as they come, as the machines of RecordIO.generate_read_record do, to the end of the source, and never sees where one read from the source ends and the next begins.\n\nA record of type R{Borrowed} borrows the bytes of stream; one of type R{Owned} copies them. A package defines the parser of its views, and that of its owned records follows: where RecordIO.borrowed_type(T) is another type, the parser of T is that of the views, with each record copied. A parser that returns a record or something else, such as nothing, is @inline, so that the reader splits the union where it calls it, rather than allocating the tuple for each record, as the parsers of RecordIO.generate_read_record are. RecordIO.generate_read_record generates a parser from an Automa state machine, and RecordIO.LengthPrefixed one from a decoder of length-prefixed binary records.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.read_record"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.record_error-Tuple{RecordReader, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.record_error(reader, cause) -> Exception\n\nThe error of the record that reader last read, for a cause found after it was read, such as a check of its values: what RecordIO.exception of its type makes of its RecordIO.offset, its RecordIO.number and cause, as the reader throws for a malformed record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.record_error"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.records-Tuple{RecordIO.AbstractReader}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.records(reader::RecordIO.AbstractReader{T}) -> RecordReader{T}\n\nThe reader of records that reader wraps; of a RecordReader, the reader itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.records"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.remaining-Tuple{RecordReader}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.remaining(reader::RecordReader) -> AbstractBufReader\n\nThe BufferIO reader of the bytes of reader that it has not read as records, from where it is, to the end of its source.\n\nFormats that start with a header in another form read it from here before their records, as BAM files do, and formats whose records another format follows read that part from here, as GFF3 files hand a trailing FASTA section to a FASTA reader. It reads the buffer that reader parses from, after the last record read, so reading from it is a read of the source, after which a record that borrows the buffer isn't valid. read, readline, peek, eof and the other functions of BufferIO readers read it, and BufferIO.IOReader wraps it as an IO. Reading records may alternate with reading from it, and the offsets in later errors count the bytes read from it too, so that they are offsets in the source, past a header read from here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.remaining"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.separated-Tuple{AbstractVector{UInt8}, UnitRange{Int64}, UInt8}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.separated(data::AbstractVector{UInt8}, range::UnitRange{Int}, delimiter::UInt8)\n\nIterate over the ranges of the values of the field of data in range, which delimiter separates, empty ones included, allocating nothing. An empty field has one empty value, and a field that ends with delimiter an empty last value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.separated"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.skip_while-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Any, Int64}} where F","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.skip_while(f, iterator, p::Int) -> Int\n\nThe position of the first byte of iterator from position p for which f is false, or the position after its last byte, where iterate(iterator, p) gives the byte at position p.\n\nThe machines of RecordIO.generate_execute_code with skip = true call it in a state that loops to itself on a set of bytes, such as a long sequence of a SAM record, with f the test of the set, which has no branches. A RecordIO.Stream tests 32 bytes at a time, which compiles to vector instructions, and reads more of the source when it reaches the end of the bytes in its buffer; any other iterator, one byte at a time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.skip_while"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#RecordIO.try_parse_field","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.try_parse_field(T, data::AbstractVector{UInt8}, range::UnitRange{Int}) -> Union{T, Nothing}\n\nThe field of data in range as a number of type T, as RecordIO.parse_field reads it, or nothing where parse_field throws: for an integer that T does not hold, an integer without digits, or a float that isn't one. An accessor reads a field with parse_field, and a check of the field with this, which enters no try. A range outside data throws as indexing data there does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.try_parse_field"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.validate-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.validate(record) -> record\nRecordIO.validate(reader) -> Int\nRecordIO.validate(R, source) -> Int\n\nCheck that a record, or every record of a file, honours the specification of its format, and throw an ArgumentError that says how the first that doesn't breaks it.\n\nOf a record, it returns the record, which RecordIO.problem has nothing to say of.\nOf a reader of a record package, it reads the records that are left, and returns how many it checked; the package defines it, with the loop below.\nOf a type of reader R <: RecordIO.AbstractReader and a source, it opens the source with R, checks it, and closes it, as in validate(Reader, \"records.txt\").\n\nA reader does not check records as it reads them, so a file that is new is checked once, and then read as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.validate"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.validate-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, RecordReader, Any}} where F","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.validate(step, reader::RecordReader, state) -> Int\n\nCheck the records that reader has left, and return how many it checked: the loop by which a record package checks a file.\n\nstep(state, record) checks a record, with what the records before it left in state, such as the number of fields that every record of a file has, and returns:\n\nthe next state, for a record that honours the specification;\na String that says how the record breaks it, which is thrown as the error of the record, RecordIO.record_error, with its number and its offset in the source;\nRecordIO.Stop(), where the records end before the source does, as at a section in another format.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.validate"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.write_bytes-Tuple{Any, DenseVector{UInt8}}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.write_bytes(sink, bytes) -> Int\nRecordIO.write_bytes(sink, bytes, range::UnitRange{Int}) -> Int\n\nWrite bytes, a vector or a memory view of bytes, or those of range of it, to sink, and return their number.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.write_bytes"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.write_le-Tuple{Any, Union{Int8, UInt8}}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.write_le(sink, x::Integer) -> Int\n\nWrite the bytes of x, an integer of a fixed size, in little-endian order, and return their number.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.write_le"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.write_length_prefixed-Tuple{Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.write_length_prefixed(sink, parts...) -> Int\n\nWrite the record that parts, vectors or memory views of bytes, make together, after its length in bytes as a little-endian Int32, as RecordIO.LengthPrefixed reads it, and return the number of bytes written, the 4 of the length included.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.write_length_prefixed"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#RecordIO.write_line-Tuple{Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.write_line(sink, bytes) -> Int\nRecordIO.write_line(sink, bytes, range::UnitRange{Int}) -> Int\n\nWrite bytes, as RecordIO.write_bytes does, and a newline, and return the number of bytes written.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.write_line"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#RecordIO.write_record","page":"Reference","text":"RecordIO.write_record(sink, record) -> Int\n\nWrite record to sink, with what ends or frames it in its format, such as the newline of a line, and return the number of bytes written.\n\nA record package defines it for its record type, as it defines RecordIO.read_record to read one, and for anything else that its files hold, such as the lines of a header. RecordIO.write_bytes, RecordIO.write_line, RecordIO.write_le and RecordIO.write_length_prefixed write to any sink, and allocate nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RecordIO.write_record"},{"category":"section","location":"reading/#Reading-records","page":"Reading records","text":"A RecordReader{T} reads records of type T from a source, with the parser of the record type, and is an iterator of them, as a Vector{T} is. The examples read lines with the Line record type of Record types, whose parser gives nothing for a comment, which the reader skips.\n\ntext_of_lines = join([\"# a header\", \"first\", \"second\", \"# between\", \"third\"], '\\n') * '\\n'\nreader = RecordReader{Line{Owned}}(IOBuffer(text_of_lines))\neltype(reader), [String(text(line)) for line in reader]\n(Main.Line{RecordIO.Owned}, [\"first\", \"second\", \"third\"])\n\nIteration ends at the end of the source, and close(reader) closes the source.","title":"Reading records"},{"category":"section","location":"reading/#Owned-records-and-views","page":"Reading records","text":"The record type says what iteration yields:\n\nR{Owned}: records of their own, each with a copy of its bytes, which stay as they are after the next record is read;\nR{Borrowed}: views of the reader's buffer, which allocate nothing for each, and are valid until the next record is read.\n\nThe owned records are copies of the views, so iterating over a reader of R{Owned} is Iterators.map(copy, views). Reading a field as a view, such as text, allocates nothing either, so what a reader of views allocates is the reader itself, with the buffer of its source, whatever the number of records:\n\ntotal_length(reader) = sum(line -> ncodeunits(text(line)), reader)\nviews(lines) = RecordReader{LineView}(IOBuffer(lines))\ntotal_length(views(text_of_lines)) # compile it\nfew, many = text_of_lines, text_of_lines^100\n(@allocated total_length(views(few))) == (@allocated total_length(views(many)))\nfalse","title":"Owned records and views"},{"category":"section","location":"reading/#Keeping-records","page":"Reading records","text":"collect copies every view, so that a collected vector holds owned records, of RecordIO.owned_type of the reader's type:\n\ncollected = collect(views(text_of_lines))\neltype(collected), String.(text.(collected))\n(Main.Line{RecordIO.Owned}, [\"first\", \"second\", \"third\"])\n\nA comprehension, map or push! of views keeps views of bytes that later reads move or overwrite, so copy each as it comes to keep it:\n\ncopies = [copy(line) for line in views(text_of_lines)]\nString.(text.(copies))\n3-element Vector{String}:\n \"first\"\n \"second\"\n \"third\"","title":"Keeping records"},{"category":"section","location":"reading/#Who-owns-which-bytes","page":"Reading records","text":"Each buffer has one owner, and is valid while its owner leaves it as it is:\n\nBuffer Owner Valid\nthe bytes of the parser's stream the buffer of the reader's source until the next record is read: consuming a record and refilling the buffer move its bytes, and growing it replaces its memory\na view the buffer of the reader's source until the next record is read\nan owned record's bytes the record as long as the record; copy(view) copies the bytes of the record, and no more\nthe source the reader from when the reader is made until it is closed; read it only through the reader, or RecordIO.remaining\n\nThe bytes are Julia memory, and a view keeps its memory alive, so a view kept too long doesn't read freed memory: it reads the bytes that its owner has put there since.","title":"Who owns which bytes"},{"category":"section","location":"reading/#Reading-one-record-at-a-time","page":"Reading records","text":"RecordIO.read_next(reader) reads the next record, skipping what isn't one, and returns nothing at the end of the source. Iteration reads through it, and it is how a package reads records itself, as a query of an indexed file does, which stops at the first record past its region.\n\nreader = RecordReader{LineView}(IOBuffer(\"first\\n# a trailing comment\\n\"))\nfirst_line = RecordIO.read_next(reader)\nString(text(first_line)), RecordIO.read_next(reader)\n(\"first\", nothing)","title":"Reading one record at a time"},{"category":"section","location":"reading/#The-readers-of-a-package","page":"Reading records","text":"A package's reader is parameterised on the records it yields, Reader{T} <: RecordIO.AbstractReader{T}, as a Vector{T} is on its elements, with a field records, the RecordReader{T} of its source, which gives it iteration, eltype and collect: RecordIO.AbstractReader. So Reader{Record{Owned}} yields owned records and Reader{Record{Borrowed}} views, and collect gives owned records either way.\n\nstruct LineReader{T, R <: RecordReader{T}} <: RecordIO.AbstractReader{T}\n    records::R\nend\n\nLineReader{T}(source) where {T} = (records = RecordReader{T}(source); LineReader{T, typeof(records)}(records))\n\nreader = LineReader{LineView}(IOBuffer(text_of_lines))\neltype(reader), eltype(collect(reader))\n(Main.Line{RecordIO.Borrowed}, Main.Line{RecordIO.Owned})\n\nA reader that ends its records before the end of its source, as one of a format whose records another format follows does, defines iterate itself, with RecordIO.read_next of its records, and collect then gives what it yields.","title":"The readers of a package"},{"category":"section","location":"reading/#The-bytes-that-aren't-records","page":"Reading records","text":"RecordIO.remaining(reader) is the BufferIO reader of the bytes that the reader hasn't read as records, from where it is: the buffer that the reader parses from. A format that starts with a header in another form reads it from there before its records, as BAM files do:\n\nreader = RecordReader{Line{Owned}}(IOBuffer(\"HEADER:4\\nfirst\\nsecond\\n\"))\nheader = readline(RecordIO.remaining(reader))\nheader, [String(text(line)) for line in reader]\n(\"HEADER:4\", [\"first\", \"second\"])\n\nReading from it moves the reader on, so the records continue after the bytes read, and reading from it may alternate with reading records.\n\nA format that ends in another one stops reading records where the other starts, and reads the rest from there, as GFF3 files hand a trailing FASTA section to a FASTA reader:\n\nreader = RecordReader{Line{Owned}}(IOBuffer(\"first\\nsecond\\nEND\\nanother format\\n\"))\nlines = String[]\nwhile (line = RecordIO.read_next(reader)) !== nothing && text(line) != \"END\"\n    push!(lines, String(text(line)))\nend\nlines, read(RecordIO.remaining(reader), String)\n([\"first\", \"second\"], \"another format\\n\")","title":"The bytes that aren't records"},{"category":"section","location":"sources/#Sources","page":"Sources","text":"A reader parses its records straight from where their bytes are, which depends on the source:\n\na vector of bytes that are contiguous in memory, or a regular file opened with open, is parsed as one view of its bytes, the file mapped into memory;\nany other IO, such as an IOBuffer, is buffered with a BufReader, which the reader grows as the parser needs;\na BufferIO reader is parsed from its own buffer.\n\nThe bytes are copied only into an owned record and, for an IO that isn't a regular file, into the BufReader's buffer, so a reader of views reads its source without allocating for each record.","title":"Sources"},{"category":"section","location":"sources/#Bytes-in-memory","page":"Sources","text":"reader = RecordReader{Line{Owned}}(Vector{UInt8}(\"first\\nsecond\\n\"))\n[String(text(line)) for line in reader]\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"first\"\n \"second\"\n\nA vector is parsed where its bytes are, so they need to be contiguous in memory: a vector of a type of which MemoryViews makes a view, such as a Vector{UInt8}, a contiguous view of one, or the codeunits of a String. Any other vector, such as a strided view or a range, throws an ArgumentError; read a copy of it, as a Vector{UInt8}, instead.\n\nA regular file opened with open is mapped into memory and parsed the same, so its bytes are read as the parser reaches them, and never copied into a buffer.","title":"Bytes in memory"},{"category":"section","location":"sources/#IOs","page":"Sources","text":"reader = RecordReader{Line{Owned}}(IOBuffer(\"first\\nsecond\\n\"))\n[String(text(line)) for line in reader]\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"first\"\n \"second\"\n\nThe BufReader starts with BufferIO's buffer size for the IO, and grows for a record longer than it. To choose the size, pass a BufReader of your own. A buffer of 4 bytes makes the lines span many fills, but the parser never sees where one fill ends and the next begins, since its stream grows the buffer and reads on until the line is complete:\n\nusing BufferIO: BufReader\nreader = RecordReader{Line{Owned}}(BufReader(IOBuffer(\"a longer first line\\nsecond\\n\"), 4))\n[String(text(line)) for line in reader]\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"a longer first line\"\n \"second\"\n\nA decompressing stream of TranscodingStreams, such as CodecZlib.GzipDecompressorStream, is an IO too. BufferIO can't read it directly, since two methods of readbytes! apply to it, so with TranscodingStreams loaded, a reader reads it through an adapter of its own. Its bytes are copied once more than a BufferIO reader's would be: from the stream's buffer into the BufReader's.","title":"IOs"},{"category":"section","location":"sources/#BufferIO-readers","page":"Sources","text":"A BufferIO reader is parsed from its own buffer, such as a CursorReader of bytes in memory:\n\nusing BufferIO: CursorReader\nreader = RecordReader{Line{Owned}}(CursorReader(\"first\\nsecond\\n\"))\n[String(text(line)) for line in reader]\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"first\"\n \"second\"\n\nA record needs to be whole in the buffer, so a reader whose buffer can't grow, such as one that holds one block of a compressed file, is wrapped with BufferIO's expandable. The wrapper forwards the reader's buffer, and copies bytes into a buffer of its own only for a record that spans the end of it:\n\nusing BufferIO.Testing: BoundedReader\nsource = BoundedReader(\"a longer first line\\nsecond\\n\", 8) # a buffer of at most 8 bytes\nreader = RecordReader{Line{Owned}}(source)\n[String(text(line)) for line in reader]\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"a longer first line\"\n \"second\"\n\nA package makes its own kind of source readable by making it a BufferIO reader: a subtype of AbstractBufReader, with methods of get_buffer, fill_buffer, consume and close, as BufferIO's documentation describes. It may also say where a record is in errors, with a method of RecordIO.offset, as the Errors page shows.","title":"BufferIO readers"},{"category":"section","location":"sources/#Taking-over-the-source","page":"Sources","text":"The reader takes over its source: read from it only through the reader, or through RecordIO.remaining, which is the BufferIO reader that the reader parses from. close(reader) closes it, and so the source.","title":"Taking over the source"},{"category":"section","location":"errors/#Errors","page":"Errors","text":"A parser says that a record is malformed by returning a negative count, rather than by throwing, so that reading a well-formed record enters no try. The reader then asks why, with RecordIO.malformation(T, stream), which may parse the record again, and throws the error of the record type, RecordIO.exception(T), with where the record is.","title":"Errors"},{"category":"section","location":"errors/#A-record-type's-errors","page":"Errors","text":"By default, the error is a RecordIO.RecordError, with the offset of the record in the source, the number of the parse, and the cause. A record of a number, whose parser rejects anything else:\n\nstruct Value{O <: Ownership}\n    bytes::ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}\nend\n\n@inline function RecordIO.read_record(::Type{Value{Borrowed}}, stream::RecordIO.Stream)\n    line, n = RecordIO.read_record(LineView, stream)\n    line === nothing && return nothing, n\n    all(byte -> UInt8('0') <= byte <= UInt8('9'), line.bytes) || return nothing, -1\n    return Value{Borrowed}(line.bytes), n\nend\n\nRecordIO.malformation(::Type{Value{Borrowed}}, stream::RecordIO.Stream) = ArgumentError(\"the value is not a number\")\n\ntry\n    collect(RecordReader{Value{Borrowed}}(IOBuffer(\"12\\n# a comment\\nthirteen\\n\")))\ncatch error\n    error\nend\nRecordIO.RecordError{Int64}(15, 3, ArgumentError(\"the value is not a number\"))\n\nThe number counts every parse, what isn't a record, such as the comment, included, and the offset is that of the start of the record.\n\nA package gives its records its own errors with RecordIO.MalformedRecord, which turns a cause of the types it names into an error with the package's message, at no cost per record:\n\nRecordIO.exception(::Type{<:Value}) = RecordIO.MalformedRecord{ArgumentError}(\"Malformed value\")\n\ntry\n    collect(RecordReader{Value{Borrowed}}(IOBuffer(\"12\\n# a comment\\nthirteen\\n\")))\ncatch error\n    error\nend\nArgumentError(\"Malformed value 3 at offset 15: the value is not a number\")","title":"A record type's errors"},{"category":"section","location":"errors/#Where-a-record-is","page":"Errors","text":"The offset of an error is RecordIO.offset(source, bytes): by default, the number of bytes of the source before the record, counted from where reading started, and those read through RecordIO.remaining, such as a header, are counted too. A source of a package's own gives its own kind of offset with a method for its type, as a source of the chunks of a BGZF file gives virtual offsets. It is called only when a record fails, so it costs nothing per record.","title":"Where a record is"},{"category":"section","location":"errors/#The-end-of-the-source","page":"Errors","text":"A record that the source ends inside is truncated, which is an error of the record type too. A parser that reads lines accepts a last line without a newline, and one of length-prefixed records, RecordIO.LengthPrefixed, throws for a record shorter than its length says:\n\nstruct Blob{O <: Ownership}\n    bytes::ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}\nend\n\ndecode_blob(::Type{Blob{Borrowed}}, bytes) = Blob{Borrowed}(bytes[5:end])\n\nRecordIO.read_record(::Type{Blob{Borrowed}}, stream::RecordIO.Stream) = RecordIO.LengthPrefixed(decode_blob)(Blob{Borrowed}, stream)\nRecordIO.malformation(::Type{Blob{Borrowed}}, stream::RecordIO.Stream) = RecordIO.malformation(RecordIO.LengthPrefixed(decode_blob), Blob{Borrowed}, stream)\n\n# A length of 10 bytes, and 3 bytes after it.\ntruncated = [reinterpret(UInt8, [htol(Int32(10))]); codeunits(\"abc\")]\ntry\n    collect(RecordReader{Blob{Borrowed}}(IOBuffer(truncated)))\ncatch error\n    error\nend\nRecordIO.RecordError{Int64}(0, 1, ErrorException(\"the record is truncated\"))","title":"The end of the source"},{"category":"section","location":"errors/#Parsers-from-machines","page":"Errors","text":"The parsers of RecordIO.generate_read_record quote the record and give the byte where the machine fails, with RecordIO.malformed_record, and an ArgumentError that the record expression throws while checking gains the quoted record too.","title":"Parsers from machines"}]
}
